// design-system.jsonc: the design system and agentic composition: its decisions and the requirements its models are held to (an area part: items whose id is ds- and a number, or starts d-ds-; docs/design-system.md).
// A part of the Strata specification; spec.jsonc lists the parts. Generated by scripts/split-spec.mjs
// the first time, edited in place after that. See specifications/README.md.
{
  "decisions": [
    {"id":"d-ds-1","ref":"D-DS-1","title":"A neutral base design system, themed by tokens, with Fieldnotes as one theme","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**A neutral base design system, themed by tokens, with Fieldnotes as one theme.** The platform draws every tenant with one neutral design system whose components read semantic tokens only. A tenant themes it by token values alone; Fieldnotes' editorial look becomes one theme among others, not the platform's look."},
    {"id":"d-ds-2","ref":"D-DS-2","title":"Tenants arrange their layouts, and a layout is data","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Tenants arrange their layouts, and a layout is data.** A tenant arranges the regions and widgets of its screens. The arrangement is data the tenant edits, checked by the platform, never code."},
    {"id":"d-ds-3","ref":"D-DS-3","title":"Agentic first","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Agentic first.** The platform is built to be composed by agents: an agent reads the backend specification and the frontend specification, composes the data and the screens a tenant asks for, configures them, and the tenant watches each step being applied live."},
    {"id":"d-ds-4","ref":"D-DS-4","title":"Wireframing is a first-class concept","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Wireframing is a first-class concept.** An agent first produces a low-fidelity structural wireframe (regions, templates, pattern placeholders that will be bound to data, possibly before the data model exists), which renders at once, and refines it step by step to full fidelity. Each step is a valid state the tenant can see."},
    {"id":"d-ds-5","ref":"D-DS-5","title":"Public and private for every element, and nothing the data layer would refuse","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Public and private for every element, and nothing the data layer would refuse.** Every element of the interface (an app, a page, a template region, a widget, a pattern instance, a field rendering, a navigation entry, a wireframe placeholder) is public or private to an audience, levels compose, and work in progress has its own audience. The interface never discloses what the data layer would refuse, including the existence of a hidden element, the label of a region left empty, counts and placeholders."},
    {"id":"d-ds-6","ref":"D-DS-6","title":"Role-based views","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Role-based views.** One page is seen by each person according to their roles: which widgets, regions, fields, actions and navigation entries appear, and whether an action is shown, disabled or hidden, follow the same policy and invoker evaluation the server uses, never a separate client rule. An admin control panel is seen by tenant admins only, by construction."},
    {"id":"d-ds-7","ref":"D-DS-7","title":"One machine-readable catalogue, with a composition tier and a governed extension path (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**One machine-readable catalogue, with a composition tier and a governed extension path.** specifications/ui-catalogue.jsonc lists tokens, themes, fonts, primitives, layout containers, patterns, blocks, templates, shells, bindings, gestures, display types, sample labels and codes, each with a JSON Schema of its props, accepted examples and refused examples with their codes. A product may define composite patterns in a static tree language: node kinds from a closed list, slot references that are paths of at most three segments, conditionals on presence, emptiness or equality to a literal, iteration over a list slot or rows, no functions, no recursion, a bound on expanded nodes, so it is not Turing-complete; the kernel refuses a composite with an unnamed control. Renderer registry entries are data (id, version, module hash, capabilities); catalogue gaps have a status lifecycle and a resolved-by link."},
    {"id":"d-ds-8","ref":"D-DS-8","title":"Primitives are native elements with class contracts, and custom elements only for behaviour natives lack (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Primitives are native elements with class contracts, and custom elements only for behaviour natives lack.** A primitive with a native element is that element with a ds-* class contract; a custom element is used only for dialog, sheet, menu, tabs, tooltip, toast region, the record picker (form-associated through ElementInternals) and the gesture hosts. Every primitive renders into the light DOM, a deliberate one-way choice: page styles and ARIA references reach every element, and no element can encapsulate its styles. This makes D-FE-4 precise."},
    {"id":"d-ds-9","ref":"D-DS-9","title":"Display types are declared per object type, and the server resolves people and reference titles (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Display types are declared per object type, and the server resolves people and reference titles.** A presentation element per object type names its title field and a display type per field; a widget may override it. The gateway resolves principals to display names under the tenant's member-name setting and includes referenced records under the reader's policies, and no longer sends principal strings. A field with no display type is drawn by a fallback of its type that never draws a record id, an epoch number or a JSON object."},
    {"id":"d-ds-10","ref":"D-DS-10","title":"Drafts are unpublished product versions outside the tenant's state (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Drafts are unpublished product versions outside the tenant's state.** A draft is the next version of a product (source, compiled bundle, interface drafts), held in a per-tenant draft store outside the tenant's state, keyed by product and draft id with the base version it was started from (one draft per product by default; merging drafts is out of scope and a stale base is refused naming the conflicts), written by compare-and-swap on its revision, with each write recording its actor. The draft store has a size quota, retention, encryption at rest, backup, tenant export, deletion at offboarding and subject erasure over draft sources. A preview is built from a viewer-scoped snapshot of the live tenant holding only the records the viewer may read under the live schema, each redacted by its field read rules; draft filters, policy conditions and conversions run only on that snapshot, draft policies only narrow, and references are followed inside it. An agent's preview uses samples unless the person grants live reads for the session. Drafts across products and the tenant's interface draft can form a draft set, previewed, approved and applied together. Discarding deletes the draft."},
    {"id":"d-ds-11","ref":"D-DS-11","title":"Screens are for whom they are declared for; derived audiences only narrow and lint (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Screens are for whom they are declared for; derived audiences only narrow and lint.** Every element declares an audience in roles or groups. A viewer is served an element when every declared audience above and on it holds them and its derived data audience does. Admin elements are refused for members, the public, and any audience naming a role held by everyone by construction (any identity provider's default role, all-members teams, roles that standing invitation templates hand out); a role all current members merely hold is warned about, and the assignment that makes an admin-named role universal asks the owner to confirm. Own scope is derived from a condition mentioning principal. Served parts carry no declared audiences or group names. The server marks withheld values only for required fields."},
    {"id":"d-ds-12","ref":"D-DS-12","title":"Roles vary one composition through declared audiences, ordered variants and advisory affordances (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Roles vary one composition through declared audiences, ordered variants and advisory affordances.** A tenant's shells are chosen by the first audience that holds the viewer; a route's screen variants by the first whose audience holds the viewer and that serves them something. An action is absent when its invokers exclude the viewer; per row, a preflight answers allowed, refused or unknown and is advisory; an admin action whose preflight is unknown is drawn disabled. Builders preview drafts and roles on a route of its own; ui-build grants reads of interface and schema definitions and the preview, and no reads of live data beyond the builder's own."},
    {"id":"d-ds-13","ref":"D-DS-13","title":"A tenant's overlay is its own layer, orphaned per widget check, with the product base as the fallback (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**A tenant's overlay is its own layer, orphaned per widget check, with the product base as the fallback.** The tenant's overlay of a product's screen (ui-layout) addresses widgets, containers and regions by id; precedence is catalogue, then product base, then overlay; overlays, groups and the shell list are drafted in the tenant's interface draft and published by the owner. A product upgrade orphans overlay entries that fail under the new catalogue and schema; a tenant's move to a new rendering major may also orphan base widgets of products published before it. Orphans are reported per tenant and notified; an audience left with nothing by orphaned overlay entries is served the product's base screen; automatic drops are recorded and reversible."},
    {"id":"d-ds-14","ref":"D-DS-14","title":"Agents build by writing drafts; the owner publishes an immutable approved revision (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Agents build by writing drafts; the owner publishes an immutable approved revision.** An agent writes a product's draft by compare-and-swap on its revision under a delegation scoped to that draft, one agent per draft by convention with an advisory lease as the signal. Submitting snapshots the revision immutably with a content hash; approval shows the effective-permission delta first, and a delta that opens anything to the public or all members needs the owner's explicit confirmation. The owner publishes with step-up through a contract that takes the revision id and hash from the draft store, never a bundle in the request, refuses a hash mismatch, re-plans against the live tenant, always passes the planned-against schema version, re-plans once without new step-up on a schema-version race when the diff is unchanged, and otherwise refuses with product.base-moved. Builders and agents see diffs without live record counts; counts go only to the owner at approval."},
    {"id":"d-ds-15","ref":"D-DS-15","title":"Themes are typed seeds, derived once and stored; new pairs never restyle silently (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Themes are typed seeds, derived once and stored; new pairs never restyle silently.** A tenant theme stores its seeds, derivation version, derived set and hash; later tokens have per-version defaults; a contrast pair added later that a stored theme fails is reported as non-blocking until the owner's next theme step, and any change to a stored theme is a new derivation version with a record and an undo. Contrast is checked at definition; values reach the page through the CSSOM."},
    {"id":"d-ds-16","ref":"D-DS-16","title":"Product versions are ordered apart from platform releases, and large upgrades stage automatically (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Product versions are ordered apart from platform releases, and large upgrades stage automatically.** Product versions are compared by semantic versioning with pre-release ordering; platform releases keep their own comparison. The 200-change cap of one schema change (KRN-1283) stays: an approved revision over it is split by the planner into dependency-ordered stages each within the cap, approved once as one revision and applied as a resumable staged job; every policy, role permission, invoker set, trigger, automation and interface element goes into the last stage, so entities created by earlier stages are unreachable to members and API callers until it commits."},
    {"id":"d-ds-17","ref":"D-DS-17","title":"Structured bindings with server contracts, and an interaction vocabulary with pure projections (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Structured bindings with server contracts, and an interaction vocabulary with pure projections.** Each binding kind has a server contract with bounds and windows. A grouped binding takes a closed field, or a reference field whose lanes the product declares as a section of their own, read under the viewer's policies, with a catch-all lane for cards whose lane the viewer may not see. Gestures carry typed arguments and pure projections; ranks are fractional with a rebalance rule and a tiebreak by id. Form-builder answers are typed per input type from the first version. Collaborative editing is a named gap."},
    {"id":"d-ds-18","ref":"D-DS-18","title":"International, accessible and responsive by rule (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**International, accessible and responsive by rule.** Tokens and layout classes use logical properties only, the root carries the direction of the request language, and every string prop is localisable from the first catalogue version. Redraws keep stable keys, restore focus and announce a builder's step once without moving focus. Primitives honour forced colours, keep targets at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels at every density (44 in touch shells), reflow at 320 CSS pixels wide, and respect safe areas."},
    {"id":"d-ds-19","ref":"D-DS-19","title":"The rendering contract is versioned per tenant, majors are judged objectively, migrations are reversible for a grace period (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**The rendering contract is versioned per tenant, majors are judged objectively, migrations are reversible for a grace period.** A tenant's rendering major is tenant configuration; compositions record the catalogue hash and contract version; products target the lower supported major and are checked against both. Majors are decided by the gate on goldens; every shipped major is gated and patched. Manifest entries name the highest catalogue minor per screen; a newer minor is announced for reload only once the new shell is installed by the service worker, and stored parts above a rolled-back shell's minor are purged and fetched again online and drawn from older stored parts offline. A reversal to the grace major restores the recorded before-state, re-checks installed products under that major with orphaning, and keeps later overlay and theme edits only where they pass under it."},
    {"id":"d-ds-20","ref":"D-DS-20","title":"Per-role shells and screen variants, first match wins","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Per-role shells and screen variants, first match wins.** A tenant lists shells in priority order, each with an audience; the first whose audience holds the viewer is used. A route may have an ordered list of screen variants by audience, first match wins, each with its own template and content check. Precedence is the declared order, never a union."},
    {"id":"d-ds-21","ref":"D-DS-21","title":"Audience groups","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Audience groups.** An audience names groups, each a set of roles defined once. The serving cap applies to distinct groups rather than raw roles; the number of roles is unbounded."},
    {"id":"d-ds-22","ref":"D-DS-22","title":"Rendering-major lifecycle: warn, then migrate","group":"Design system","decided":"2026-11-22","owner":"owner","source":"Owner decision of 2026-11-22, recorded in WG-5457.","statement":"**Rendering-major lifecycle: warn, then migrate.** The platform supports the current and the previous rendering major. Before a tenant drops out of support, its owner is warned in the builder with a preview of their real pages under the new look and a deadline; after the deadline the tenant is moved automatically, the move is recorded, and it can be reversed within the support window."},
    {"id":"d-ds-23","ref":"D-DS-23","title":"A screen's audience is an explicit declaration; the derivation lints (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**A screen's audience is an explicit declaration; the derivation lints.** Who a screen, region or element is for is always declared in roles or audience groups by its author, and owner-intended; the platform never infers it from data audiences. The derivation from policies, conditions, filters and read rules narrows what is served and produces warnings, and refuses only what is unsafe by construction: an admin element or an element with an administrative action declared for members or the public."},
    {"id":"d-ds-24","ref":"D-DS-24","title":"Interface classes are formed from signed-in status and deciding groups, and all observable hashes from the narrowed result (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Interface classes are formed from signed-in status and deciding groups, and all observable hashes from the narrowed result.** A viewer's interface class for a screen is whether they are signed in and the screen's deciding groups they are in; builder status is not part of it, because builders see drafts only through the preview route. The gateway caches the class part and narrows it per request by the viewer's data audiences; the manifest the viewer receives, its content hash, the notices it triggers and the part guard are all computed on the narrowed result. The per-screen group cap bounds the classes for that reason."},
    {"id":"d-ds-25","ref":"D-DS-25","title":"Provisional reserved entries for human tasks, process timelines, and a platform bar (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Provisional reserved entries for human tasks, process timelines, and a platform bar.** The catalogue reserves a tasks platform source, task-inbox and decision-form patterns and a decide action kind for WG-5496, and a process-timeline pattern for WG-5507 that allows branches and iterations; reserved entries are provisional (exempt from compatibility rules while refused in published compositions). Every shell variant, none included, reserves a platform bar only the platform fills, for acting as another person (serving exactly the effective principal's class, never the actor's rights), for previewing as a role set (distinguishable), and for the reload notice."},
    {"id":"d-ds-26","ref":"D-DS-26","title":"Interface class keys reach roles only through group membership (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Interface class keys reach roles only through group membership.** Team membership and time-bound grants reach a class key only through the roles roles_of gives and the groups those roles fall in; principal attributes, the clock and conditions naming principal never enter it, and act only in per-request narrowing and data reads. Interface caches are keyed by a hash over a closed list of schema fields that excludes teams and invitations; team changes and time-bound grant boundaries raise the access generation of the principals concerned."},
    {"id":"d-ds-27","ref":"D-DS-27","title":"Display types are neutral to the representation of numbers and dates (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Display types are neutral to the representation of numbers and dates.** number and money format an exact decimal string, never a floating-point number, and take currency, scale and rounding from the field type once the value algebra has them (WG-5494), from props until then; date formats a civil date as itself; each display type's accepted field types are additive."},
    {"id":"d-ds-28","ref":"D-DS-28","title":"Actions are a tagged union from the first version, and form answers are typed answer records per input type (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Actions are a tagged union from the first version, and form answers are typed answer records per input type.** The ui-action type becomes a union with a kind member defaulting to operation. A record-defined form's answers are written as answer records of platform answer types, one per input type, with a reference answer type per object type the product declares forms may reference, a file answer type whose per-question limits are checked by the write-answers step, and personal and restricted variants that per-question privacy chooses between; number and date answers use int with a scale and time until the value algebra has decimals and civil dates (WG-5494)."},
    {"id":"d-ds-29","ref":"D-DS-29","title":"Draft sets apply related drafts together (proposed)","group":"Design system","source":"Proposed in docs/design-system.md (WG-5457); not yet decided by the owner.","statement":"**Draft sets apply related drafts together.** A draft set groups drafts of several products and the tenant's interface draft; it is previewed as one projection, approved as one, and applied in dependency order with the interface draft last, each product upgrade staged within the cap; if applying stops before the last stage, nothing new is reachable by members and the owner can resume or roll back by removal upgrades in reverse order. A soft launch's product and its audience group are published as one set."}
  ],
  "claims": [
    {"id":"ds-001","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the composition model, a composition that passes the composition check SHALL bind every widget only to bindings the tenant schema defines and its pattern accepts.","rationale":"An agent's step is applied only after the check, so no state the tenant can see points at missing data or binds a pattern to the wrong kind of binding.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::check_iff","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::checked_bindings_resolve"],"ref":"DS-001","section":"Compositions and wireframes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Composition.check`","traces":["d-ds-10","d-ds-4","d-8250"]},
    {"id":"ds-002","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the composition model, a publishable composition SHALL contain no placeholder.","rationale":"Publishing requires no placeholder at all, whatever the pattern, so no wireframe shape reaches a member or the public, and every data widget is bound to something that exists.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::publishable_no_placeholder","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::publishable_bound"],"ref":"DS-002","section":"Compositions and wireframes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Composition.Publishable`","traces":["d-ds-10","d-ds-4"]},
    {"id":"ds-003","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the composition model, binding the placeholders of a valid composition one at a time to bindings their patterns accept and the schema defines SHALL give a valid composition at every step that ends publishable.","rationale":"Refinement from wireframe to full fidelity is a sequence of valid states the tenant watches.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::bindAt_valid","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::bindAt_placeholders","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::refine_valid","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::refine_publishable"],"ref":"DS-003","section":"Compositions and wireframes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Composition.refine`","traces":["d-ds-4","d-ds-3"]},
    {"id":"ds-004","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the composition model, a change of schema and catalogue that removes no binding an unchanged screen names and alters no catalogue answer it relies on, with every changed screen passing the check against the new schema and catalogue, SHALL leave every screen valid.","rationale":"The kernel may check only the screens a change touches plus the removal guard; a binding carries its shape fingerprint, so a type change of a field it reads counts as a removal; a catalogue narrowing counts as a change.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::incremental_valid"],"ref":"DS-004","section":"Compositions and wireframes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Composition.incremental_valid`","traces":["d-ds-10","d-ds-14","d-ds-19"]},
    {"id":"ds-005","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the theme model, the on-colour derived for a surface of any luminance SHALL meet the WCAG 2.2 AA contrast ratio for normal text against that surface.","rationale":"A theme derived from any accent a tenant picks has readable text on it, and the derivation's ramp search always ends.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Contrast.lean::onColour_meets_aa","lean/Strata/UI/Contrast.lean::search_finds"],"ref":"DS-005","section":"Tokens and themes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Contrast.onColour`","traces":["d-ds-15","d-ds-1"]},
    {"id":"ds-006","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the theme model, a theme that passes the theme check SHALL meet the WCAG 2.2 AA contrast ratio on every pair of tokens the catalogue declares.","rationale":"Contrast is a rule the tooling enforces when a theme is defined, not a review comment.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Contrast.lean::checkTheme_iff"],"ref":"DS-006","section":"Tokens and themes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Contrast.checkTheme`","traces":["d-ds-15","d-ds-1"]},
    {"id":"ds-007","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, every element served to a viewer SHALL be in the viewer's declared and data audiences, inside containers that are all in the viewer's declared audiences.","rationale":"The server decides what the browser is sent, so nothing the viewer may not see reaches the browser to be hidden there.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::render_sound"],"ref":"DS-007","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.render`","traces":["d-ds-5","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-008","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, a node the viewer is not served SHALL leave what the viewer is served unchanged wherever it sits among its siblings.","rationale":"The existence of a hidden widget, the label of the region around it and the number of hidden elements are not disclosed, because the container is served or pruned exactly as if the node were absent.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::hidden_leaves_no_trace","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::hidden_leaves_no_trace_in"],"ref":"DS-008","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.renderAll`","traces":["d-ds-5","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-009","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, the audience derived from an operation's invokers SHALL admit a viewer exactly when the kernel's invoke check does not refuse that viewer at its invoker stage.","rationale":"An action is shown from the server's own evaluation, so the interface and the invocation cannot disagree on who may act.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::audOf_sees"],"ref":"DS-009","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.audOf`","traces":["d-ds-6","d-ds-12"]},
    {"id":"ds-010","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, a viewer SHALL be in the data audience of an object type exactly when one of the viewer's roles, the public role included, has a Read grant on it.","rationale":"Role variation of data widgets is derived from the policies, not written beside them, and a grant to the public role makes data public to anonymous visitors.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::dataAud_sees","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::public_grant_anon_sees","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::contentAud_sub"],"ref":"DS-010","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.dataAud`","traces":["d-ds-6","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-011","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, an admin element of a composition that passes the admin check SHALL be served only to a viewer holding a role, other than public and the default roles, that a strict declared audience on or above it names.","rationale":"An admin panel cannot reach members, the public, or everyone holding a default role, by construction.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::admin_needs_role","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::admin_never_to_default_member"],"ref":"DS-011","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.adminOk`","traces":["d-ds-23","d-ds-6","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-012","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, every container served to a viewer SHALL hold a served element, one that counts as content when the container needs content.","rationale":"No region, heading or box is drawn around nothing, and a static introduction does not keep alive a region whose widgets are all hidden.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::served_has_element","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::served_needs_content"],"ref":"DS-012","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.boxOut`","traces":["d-ds-5","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-013","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the composition model, removing from a valid screen exactly the widgets that fail under a new catalogue and schema SHALL give a screen valid under them with distinct widget ids.","rationale":"An upgrade orphans the overlay entries that no longer pass instead of being refused, whether the schema or the catalogue changed. Overlay operations other than dropping a widget are not modelled.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::orphan_valid","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::orphan_removes_only_failing","lean/Strata/UI/Composition.lean::orphan_keeps_valid"],"ref":"DS-013","section":"Compositions and wireframes","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Composition.orphan`","traces":["d-ds-13","d-ds-2"]},
    {"id":"ds-014","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, the screen variant served to a viewer SHALL be the first in declared order whose audience holds the viewer and that serves the viewer an element.","rationale":"The owner's decision D-DS-20 keeps declared order, and a viewer who may read none of an earlier variant's content gets the next variant instead of a page that is not there; shells are chosen by the first audience that holds the viewer.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::selectServed_sound","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::selectServed_first","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::select_sees","lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::select_first"],"ref":"DS-014","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.selectServed`","traces":["d-ds-20","d-ds-6"]},
    {"id":"ds-015","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the draft-store model, a draft write SHALL apply only at the draft's current revision.","rationale":"Two agents, or an agent and the owner, cannot overwrite each other's draft writes; revisions strictly increase.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::no_stale_draft_write","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::revisions_increase"],"ref":"DS-015","section":"The agent build loop","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Loop.writeAt`","traces":["d-ds-14","d-ds-3"]},
    {"id":"ds-016","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the approval model, the owner's upgrade SHALL apply exactly the approved diff of the revision snapshot whose content hash was approved, re-planned equal on the live tenant.","rationale":"The upgrade takes the revision id and hash, not a bundle, so what is applied is what was approved; a moved tenant or a different snapshot refuses it.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::approve_applies_held","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::approve_class_matches","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::approve_refused_when_moved","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::approve_refused_on_hash_mismatch"],"ref":"DS-016","section":"The agent build loop","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Loop.approve`","traces":["d-ds-14","d-ds-3"]},
    {"id":"ds-017","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the draft-store model, draft writes and discards SHALL leave the tenant's state and every member's served view unchanged.","rationale":"Drafts live outside the tenant's state, so no consumer of that state needs to know about them.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::draft_steps_frame"],"ref":"DS-017","section":"The agent build loop","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Loop.draftRun`","traces":["d-ds-10","d-ds-5"]},
    {"id":"ds-018","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the preview model, every record and field value a preview computation can observe, through any chain of references, SHALL be one the viewer may read under the live schema.","rationale":"The preview is built from a viewer-scoped, redacted snapshot, so draft filters, policies and conversions cannot leak unreadable values by repeated preview.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::snapshot_readable","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::snapshot_ignores_unreadable","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::preview_reads_within_viewer","lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::followed_within_viewer"],"ref":"DS-018","section":"The agent build loop","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Loop.snapshot`","traces":["d-ds-10","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-019","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, narrowing the class part by a viewer's data audiences SHALL give exactly what rendering the composition for that viewer gives.","rationale":"The gateway caches one part per class and narrows it per request; the manifest, hashes, notices and part guard computed on the narrowed result are therefore those of a per-viewer render.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::narrow_class_eq_render"],"ref":"DS-019","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.narrow`","traces":["d-ds-24","d-ds-11"]},
    {"id":"ds-020","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the visibility model, two viewers who agree on every declared audience of a composition SHALL get the same class part.","rationale":"The class key needs no more than what decides the declared audiences: signed-in status, the deciding groups and builder status.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Visibility.lean::renderClass_shared"],"ref":"DS-020","section":"Visibility and roles","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Visibility.renderClass`","traces":["d-ds-24","d-ds-26"]},
    {"id":"ds-021","kind":"requirement","evaluate":{"method":"proof"},"check":"test","area":"design-system","lifecycle":"active","boundary":"b-13","milestone":7,"realisation":"checked","statement":"In the staging model, the view served to members SHALL be unchanged by every stage of a staged upgrade before the last.","rationale":"Every grant and interface element is in the last stage, so a stopped or running staged upgrade shows members nothing new; that the planner places them there is held by its test.","checkedBy":["lean/Strata/UI/Loop.lean::staged_invisible_until_last"],"ref":"DS-021","section":"The agent build loop","boundaryDetail":"`Strata.UI.Loop.applyEarly`","traces":["d-ds-16","d-ds-29"]}
  ]
}
